<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phra Bang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pha Bang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Phra Bang is a gilded standing Buddha image, the palladium of the Lao monarchy, after which the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> is named. Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fa-ngum">Fa Ngum</a> brought it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> in the 14th century together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>. Twice carried off to Siam, it was returned in 1867 and is kept in the former royal palace at Luang Prabang.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phra-bang.jpg" alt="Phra Bang" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tmd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phra_Bang_Budhhalawan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Phra Bang stands 83 centimetres tall, its palms facing forward in the mudra of dispelling fear, from which its name, Royal Buddha Image in the Dispelling Fear mudra, is taken. It was cast in thong, an alloy of bronze, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. According to local lore it was made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, sometime between the 1st and 9th centuries, but the features of the image suggest a much later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The image came to Lan Xang from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fa-ngum">Fa Ngum</a>, the first monarch of Lan Xang, who ruled from 1353 to 1373. In 1359 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> king gave it to Fa Ngum, his son-in-law, to lend Buddhist legitimacy to his rule and so to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, and it was used to spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> in the new kingdom. It was kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, the former royal capital, which is named after it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Devout Buddhists have long seen the image as a symbol of the right to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, holding that only a virtuous government deserves to be the caretaker of so sacred an object. It was taken to Vientiane in 1705. In 1778 the Siamese invaded Vientiane and carried it off to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, but political upheaval and misfortune there were blamed on the statue, and it was returned to the Lao in 1782. The Siamese captured it again in 1828 and, after a similar period of upheaval, gave it back in 1867.</p><p>In 2013 the image was moved from the Lao National Museum to the Haw Pha Bang, on the grounds of the Royal Palace. Each year, on the third day of Pi Mai, the Lao New Year, it is carried in procession to Wat Mai and set in a shrine, where devout laypeople ritually bathe it during the festivities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phra Bang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lao tradition credits Fa Ngum and his Khmer queen with bringing Theravada Buddhism and Cambodian monks to the court, together with the gold Buddha image known as the <b>Phra Bang</b>, although the image probably reached the capital later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its capital, with temples such as Wat Xieng Thong and the <b>Phra Bang</b> image, was the religious heart of Lao kingship.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Kingdom of Luang Phrabang</a></li><li><blockquote>The city was renamed Luang Prabang after the <b>Phra Bang</b> Buddha image was installed there in the 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/muang-sua">Muang Sua</a></li></ul></section>
